      {
        "t": "chr",
        "v": [
          "This module classifies repository files and, for tabular data files available locally, extracts each column's type and summary statistics.",
          "#### Data Tree",
          "Where the files sit within each repository. Each file is followed by how it was classified, which study it belongs to.",
          "**Repository: https://osf.io/abcde**",
          "```\n├── data/\n│   └── study.csv     📊 data      ex1\n├── materials/\n│   └── stimulus.png  📁 materials ex1\n├── analysis.R        💻 code      ex1\n└── README.md         📄 readme\n```",
          "**Repository: https://osf.io/remot**",
          "```\n└── data/\n    └── remote.csv  📊 data ex2\n```",
          "#### Data Files",
          "::: {.panel-tabset}\n\n## study.csv\n\n\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(id = 1:20, age = c(23L, 35L, 41L, 29L, 30L, 22L, \n19L, 55L, 61L, 44L, 38L, 27L, 33L, 25L, 48L, 52L, 36L, 40L, 21L, \n26L), gender = c(\"Male\", \"Female\", \"Male\", \"Female\", \"Male\", \n\"Female\", \"Male\", \"Female\", \"Male\", \"Female\", \"Male\", \"Female\", \n\"Male\", \"Female\", \"Male\", \"Female\", \"Male\", \"Female\", \"Male\", \n\"Female\"), score = c(0.5, 1, 1.5, 2, 2.5, 3, 3.5, 4, 4.5, 5, \n5.5, 6, 6.5, 7, 7.5, 8, 8.5, 9, 9.5, 10)), row.names = c(NA, \n20L), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 10, FALSE)\n```\n\n\n:::",
          "#### Descriptives Overview\n\nWe found 0 columns with at least one missing value.",
          "::: {.panel-tabset}\n\n## study.csv\n\n\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(Column = c(\"age\", \"gender\", \"id\", \"score\"), Representation = c(\"numeric\", \n\"text\", \"text\", \"numeric\"), Level = c(\"ratio\", \"nominal\", \"nominal\", \n\"ratio\"), Concept = c(\"age\", \"gender\", \"id\", \"\"), Role = c(\"measure\", \n\"measure\", \"identifier\", \"measure\"), Unit = c(\"years\", \"\", \"\", \n\"\"), Rows = c(20L, 20L, 20L, 20L), \"% Missing\" = c(0, 0, 0, 0\n), \"N Unique\" = c(20L, 2L, 20L, 20L), Mean = c(35.25, NA, NA, \n5.25), SD = c(12.052, NA, NA, 2.958), Min = c(19, NA, NA, 0.5\n), Max = c(61, NA, NA, 10)), row.names = c(\"age\", \"gender\", \"id\", \n\"score\"), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 25, FALSE)\n```\n\n\n:::",
          "This module runs automated data-quality checks (outliers, miscoded missing values, empty and constant columns, SPSS filter variables, category casing, problematic column names, mixed text encodings, spreadsheet formatting) on the extracted data files.",
          "We examined 4 columns across 1 data file.",
          "#### Careless Responding",
          "No file could be screened for careless responding.",
          "**What this does not cover.** Only 0 of 1 data file could be screened: the rest had no detectable multi-item scale block, or too few respondents. Even in a screened file, these checks only look at multi-item rating scales — the only place where a repeated or flat answer pattern is interpretable. Single questions, open text, demographics and any item outside a detected scale block are never examined. A respondent can therefore answer most of a survey carelessly and still not be flagged here, and finding nothing is not evidence that a dataset is free of careless responding."
        ]
